= 1
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Condition on the <claim count>, defining the empty sum to be zero. <Independence> gives $\mathbb E[S\mid N=k]=k\mathbb E X_1$. By the <law of total expectation>,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb ES=\mathbb EN\,\mathbb EX_1.}
$$
For nonnegative claims this also follows from <Tonelli theorem>; for signed claims finite $\mathbb EN$ and $\mathbb E|X_1|$ ensure <integrability>. <Conditional independence> likewise gives
$$
\mathbb E[e^{tS}\mid N=k]=\prod_{j=1}^k\mathbb E[e^{tX_j}]=M_{X_1}(t)^k,
\qquad
\boxed{M_S(t)=G_N(M_{X_1}(t)).}
$$
This <random-sum transform identity> holds wherever the composed series is finite, or as an extended nonnegative <expectation>. It is not permission to use an algebraic continuation outside the convergence domain.

For the zero-based <geometric distribution>, summing the <geometric series> gives
$$
G_N(z)=\frac p{1-qz},\qquad \mathbb EN=G_N'(1)=\frac qp.
$$
The <exponential distribution> of mean $\mu$ has $M_{X_1}(t)=(1-\mu t)^{-1}$ for $t<1/\mu$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb ES=\frac{q\mu}p,\qquad
M_S(t)=\frac p{1-q/(1-\mu t)}
=\frac{p(1-\mu t)}{p-\mu t}\quad(t<p/\mu).}
$$
The stricter bound $t<p/\mu$ is necessary: the <geometric series> requires $qM_{X_1}(t)<1$. At and above $p/\mu$ that series diverges; at and above $1/\mu$ even one claim has an infinite transform. Thus the displayed rational expression represents the <moment-generating function> only on its stated domain.

Since all claims are strictly positive, $S=0$ exactly when $N=0$, so $\mathbb P(S=0)=p$. Splitting the transform gives
$$
M_S(t)=p+q\frac{p/\mu}{p/\mu-t}.
$$
Uniqueness of the <moment-generating function> identifies a <mixture distribution> with a zero atom of weight $p$ and an exponential positive component of weight $q$ and rate $p/\mu$. In particular,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(S=0)=p,\qquad f_S(x)=\frac{qp}{\mu}e^{-px/\mu}\quad(x>0).}
$$
The density integrates to $q$, not one. One can also verify it directly: conditional on $N=k\geq1$, the sum has <Erlang distribution> density $x^{k-1}e^{-x/\mu}/[\mu^k(k-1)!]$. Multiplying by $pq^k$ and summing over $k$ gives $(pq/\mu)e^{-x/\mu}e^{qx/\mu}$, the same density. This is the <zero-based geometric sum of exponential variables>.

Under <excess of loss reinsurance>, the ceded amount for each claim is $(X_j-M)_+$, and the monthly reduction is their <random sum>. The <tail integral formula for moments> gives
$$
\mathbb E(X_1-M)_+=\int_M^\infty\mathbb P(X_1>x)\,dx
=\mu e^{-M/\mu}.
$$
Conditioning on $N$ again yields
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E\sum_{j=1}^N(X_j-M)_+=\frac{q\mu}p e^{-M/\mu}.}
$$
The retained payment is $\sum_{j=1}^N\min(X_j,M)$; it is generally not $\min(S,M)$.

Under <aggregate stop loss reinsurance>, the reduction is $(S-\widetilde M)_+$. The aggregate tail is $\mathbb P(S>x)=q e^{-px/\mu}$ for $x\geq0$, so
$$
\mathbb E(S-\widetilde M)_+=\int_{\widetilde M}^\infty q e^{-px/\mu}\,dx
=\frac{q\mu}p e^{-p\widetilde M/\mu}.
$$
The prefactor is positive. Equality with the per-claim recovery is equivalent to $p\widetilde M=M$, giving the unique <reinsurance retention>
$$
\boxed{\widetilde M=\frac Mp.}
$$
This is the relation for <equal expected recoveries from excess of loss and stop loss>; since $p<1$, the aggregate <reinsurance retention> exceeds the individual <reinsurance retention>. These are reductions in claim payouts, before any reinsurance premium is deducted.

= 2
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Write $M(t)=\mathbb Ee^{tX}$, with $M(0)=1$, and differentiate $\kappa(t)=\log M(t)$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\kappa'(t)&=\frac{M'(t)}{M(t)},\\
\kappa''(t)&=\frac{M''(t)}{M(t)}-\frac{M'(t)^2}{M(t)^2},\\
\kappa'''(t)&=\frac{M'''(t)}{M(t)}-\frac{3M'(t)M''(t)}{M(t)^2}
 +\frac{2M'(t)^3}{M(t)^3}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Using $M^{(j)}(0)=\mathbb EX^j$ and $\mu=\mathbb EX$ gives
$$
\boxed{\kappa_1=\mu,\qquad\kappa_2=\mathbb EX^2-\mu^2=\operatorname{Var}X,\qquad
\kappa_3=\mathbb EX^3-3\mu\mathbb EX^2+2\mu^3=\mathbb E(X-\mu)^3.}
$$
Thus the first three <cumulants> are the mean, <variance> and third <central moment>. A finite <moment-generating function> near zero justifies these differentiations. For positive claims with only three finite ordinary <moments>, the same calculations are valid as left <derivatives> at zero of the logarithm of the <Laplace transform of a nonnegative random variable>; positive <exponential moments> are not needed for the resulting <moment> identities.

For the independent <Poisson distribution> count, the <random-sum transform identity> gives
$$
M_S(t)=\exp\{\lambda(M_{X_1}(t)-1)\},\qquad
\kappa_S(t)=\lambda(M_{X_1}(t)-1).
$$
Consequently the <compound Poisson distribution> has
$$
\kappa_{S,1}=\lambda m_1,\qquad
\kappa_{S,2}=\lambda m_2,\qquad
\kappa_{S,3}=\lambda m_3.
$$
These are raw severity <moments>, not severity <cumulants>.

The printed gamma density uses rate $\nu$, not scale. Direct integration against $e^{ty}$ gives
$$
M_Y(t)=\left(\frac\nu{\nu-t}\right)^\alpha\quad(t<\nu),\qquad
\kappa_V(t)=kt-\alpha\log(1-t/\nu).
$$
Therefore the required matching equations for the <shifted gamma distribution> are
$$
k+\frac\alpha\nu=\lambda m_1,\qquad
\frac\alpha{\nu^2}=\lambda m_2,\qquad
\frac{2\alpha}{\nu^3}=\lambda m_3.
$$
For $\lambda>0$ and nonzero positive claims with finite $m_3$, these equations give positive $\alpha,\nu$ and the unique solution
$$
\boxed{\nu=\frac{2m_2}{m_3},\qquad
\alpha=\frac{4\lambda m_2^3}{m_3^2},\qquad
k=\lambda m_1-\frac{2\lambda m_2^2}{m_3}.}
$$
This is the <three-cumulant shifted gamma approximation>. In particular, its <skewness> $2/\sqrt\alpha$ equals $m_3/(\sqrt\lambda\,m_2^{3/2})$, the <skewness> of $S$.

The competing <normal approximation to a compound Poisson aggregate> is
$$
\boxed{S\approx\mathcal N(\lambda m_1,\lambda m_2),\qquad
\mathbb P(S\leq x)\approx\Phi\!\left(\frac{x-\lambda m_1}{\sqrt{\lambda m_2}}\right).}
$$
To justify its large-count limit, let $\varphi_X$ be the claim <characteristic function>. For fixed severity law with $m_2<\infty$, its expansion is $\varphi_X(u)=1+im_1u-m_2u^2/2+o(u^2)$. The <characteristic function> of $(S-\lambda m_1)/\sqrt{\lambda m_2}$ is
$$
\exp\left\{\lambda\left[\varphi_X\left(\frac t{\sqrt{\lambda m_2}}\right)-1\right]
-\frac{it\lambda m_1}{\sqrt{\lambda m_2}}\right\}
\longrightarrow e^{-t^2/2}.
$$
Thus it converges in distribution to a standard <normal distribution> as $\lambda\to\infty$.

The <normal approximation> is simple, uses only $m_1,m_2$, and works well in the centre when the count is large and standardized <skewness> is small. It imposes symmetry, whereas positive compound-Poisson claims have positive third <cumulant>. The shifted-gamma approximation reproduces that third <cumulant> and can better describe moderate right <skewness>. It requires a finite and reliably known third <moment>, and matching three <cumulants> alone does not guarantee accurate extreme tails.

Neither continuous approximation reproduces the genuine atom $\mathbb P(S=0)=e^{-\lambda}$, which matters at small $\lambda$. The <normal distribution> also assigns <probability> $\Phi(-\sqrt\lambda m_1/\sqrt{m_2})$ to negative losses. The gamma approximation is bounded below by $k$, but **its fitted shift need not be nonnegative**: exponential claims of mean $\mu$ give $k=-\lambda\mu/3$, so it too can assign mass to negative losses. If $k>0$, it instead excludes some genuinely possible small losses. These support and atom discrepancies prevent either approximation from being a universal replacement for the exact aggregate law.

= 3
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For the <Poisson process> of claims and <relative safety loading> $\rho$, the premium rate in the <classical risk model> is $c=(1+\rho)\lambda\mu$. Dividing the <adjustment coefficient> equation $\lambda(M(r)-1)=cr$ by $\lambda$ explains why the arrival rate cancels. Interpret $r_\infty$ as the upper endpoint of the finite <moment-generating function> domain, so $M(r)<\infty$ for $0\leq r<r_\infty$ and its stated divergence occurs at that endpoint.

Set $F(r)=M(r)-1-(1+\rho)\mu r$. Then
$$
F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=-\rho\mu<0,\qquad
F''(r)=\mathbb E[X_1^2e^{rX_1}]>0\quad(0\leq r<r_\infty).
$$
Differentiation is valid inside the finite-transform domain, and its positive neighbourhood gives finite <moments> of every order. Thus $F$ is <strictly convex> and negative just to the right of zero. If $r_\infty<\infty$, divergence of $M(r)$ makes $F(r)\to\infty$ at the endpoint. If $r_\infty=\infty$, choose $a>0$ with $\mathbb P(X_1\geq a)>0$; then $M(r)\geq\mathbb P(X_1\geq a)e^{ar}$, which eventually exceeds every <linear function>. Again $F(r)\to\infty$. The <intermediate value theorem> gives a positive zero.

There is at most one positive zero: <strict convexity> and $F(0)=0$ make the secant slope $F(r)/r$ strictly increasing for $r>0$. It starts at $-\rho\mu$ and crosses zero exactly once. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{There is a unique }R\in(0,r_\infty)\text{ with }M(R)-1=(1+\rho)\mu R.}
$$
This proves the <secant-slope existence criterion for an adjustment coefficient> in the present setting.

Put $m_j=\mathbb EX_1^j$. For $u>0$, the positive remainder of the <exponential series> gives $e^u>1+u+u^2/2$. Apply it to $u=RX_1$ and take <expectations>:
$$
(1+\rho)\mu R=M(R)-1>\mu R+\frac{m_2R^2}{2}.
$$
Dividing by $R>0$ yields
$$
\boxed{R<r_1=\frac{2\rho\mu}{m_2}.}
$$
Keeping the cubic term similarly gives
$$
\rho\mu>\frac{m_2R}{2}+\frac{m_3R^2}{6}.
$$
The <polynomial> $g(r)=m_3r^2+3m_2r-6\rho\mu$ is strictly increasing for $r\geq0$, is negative at zero and tends to infinity. Let $r_2$ be its unique positive zero. The last inequality says $g(R)<0$, so
$$
\boxed{R<r_2=\frac{\sqrt{9m_2^2+24\rho\mu m_3}-3m_2}{2m_3},\qquad
g(r_2)=0.}
$$
Finally, $3m_2r_1=6\rho\mu$, whence $g(r_1)=m_3r_1^2>0$. Monotonicity gives $r_2<r_1$. Thus the requested <upper bounds> can in fact be sharpened to the strict ordering **$0<R<r_2<r_1$**. The bounds themselves need not lie in the finite-transform domain; the argument only evaluates the actual transform at $R$ and <polynomial> <moments> elsewhere. These are <polynomial moment bounds for the adjustment coefficient>.

For rate-$\alpha$ <exponential distribution> claims,
$$
\mu=\frac1\alpha,\qquad m_2=\frac2{\alpha^2},\qquad m_3=\frac6{\alpha^3},\qquad
M(r)=\frac\alpha{\alpha-r}\quad(r<\alpha).
$$
The positive root satisfies $1/(\alpha-R)=(1+\rho)/\alpha$ after dividing out $R$, so
$$
\boxed{R=\frac{\alpha\rho}{1+\rho},\qquad
r_1=\alpha\rho,\qquad
r_2=\frac\alpha2\left(\sqrt{1+4\rho}-1\right).}
$$
The cubic-bound equation reduces to $r_2^2+\alpha r_2-\rho\alpha^2=0$, selecting the displayed positive root. In particular $R<\alpha$, as required by the transform domain, for every $\rho>0$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=Paper 43/4}

A <credibility estimate> combines a risk's observed experience with collective or <prior> information, typically as $Z\overline X+(1-Z)m_0$. The <credibility factor> $Z\in[0,1]$ is the weight assigned to the individual experience: more reliable or more extensive data generally increases it. <Bayesian credibility> treats the risk parameter as random with a <prior distribution> and predicts future claims by averaging the conditional claim mean under its <posterior distribution>. Under <squared-error loss> this <posterior predictive> mean is optimal. It need not be an <affine combination> in every Bayesian model; the <Poisson-gamma conjugacy> calculation below yields that credibility form exactly, rather than as a best-linear approximation.

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $s=\sum_{j=1}^n x_j$. The conditional joint <likelihood function> of the observed counts is
$$
L(\theta;\mathbf x)=\prod_{j=1}^n\frac{e^{-\theta}\theta^{x_j}}{x_j!}
=\frac{e^{-n\theta}\theta^s}{\prod_jx_j!}.
$$
The <prior> is a <gamma distribution> with shape two and rate $\lambda$. Multiplication by its density gives the unnormalized <posterior density> $\theta^{s+1}e^{-(n+\lambda)\theta}$. Normalizing with the <gamma function> yields
$$
\pi(\theta\mid\mathbf x)=\frac{(n+\lambda)^{s+2}}{\Gamma(s+2)}
\theta^{s+1}e^{-(n+\lambda)\theta},\qquad\theta>0.
$$
Thus <Poisson-gamma conjugacy> gives $\Theta\mid\mathbf x\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(s+2,n+\lambda)$. Conditional on $\Theta$ and the past data, the next count has mean $\Theta$, so the <law of total expectation> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X_{n+1}\mid\mathbf x]=\mathbb E[\Theta\mid\mathbf x]=\frac{s+2}{n+\lambda}.}
$$
With $\overline x=s/n$ and <prior> mean $m_0=2/\lambda$, this is exactly
$$
\boxed{\frac{s+2}{n+\lambda}
=Z\overline x+(1-Z)m_0,\qquad Z=\frac n{n+\lambda}.}
$$
Here $n\geq1$, $\lambda>0$, and the <credibility factor> increases to one as the number of observed years grows. The denominator uses the <prior> rate $\lambda$ and total exposure $n$; it does not add two to the exposure when adding the <prior> shape.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Given $\Theta=\theta$, the sum of the independent yearly counts is $S_n\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(n\theta)$: their <probability generating functions> multiply to $\exp(n\theta(z-1))$. Averaging this conditional law over the <prior> gives the <mixed Poisson distribution>
$$
\boxed{p_n(s):=\mathbb P(S_n=s)
=\frac{n^s}{s!}\int_0^\infty e^{-n\theta}\theta^s\pi(\theta)\,d\theta,\qquad s\geq0.}
$$
The likelihood for the full vector depends on $\theta$ only through $s$, so $S_n$ is a <sufficient statistic>; conditioning on the total or on the full observed vector gives the same <posterior> for $\Theta$.

For an event of positive <probability> $p_n(s)>0$, the <posterior density> is proportional to $e^{-n\theta}\theta^s\pi(\theta)$. Put $I_s=\int_0^\infty e^{-n\theta}\theta^s\pi(\theta)\,d\theta$. Its mean is $I_{s+1}/I_s$. The future count has <conditional mean> $\Theta$, so
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[X_{n+1}\mid S_n=s]
&=\frac{I_{s+1}}{I_s}\\
&=\frac{(s+1)!\,p_n(s+1)/n^{s+1}}{s!\,p_n(s)/n^s}
=\boxed{\frac{(s+1)\mathbb P(S_n=s+1)}{n\mathbb P(S_n=s)}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the <posterior mean from adjacent mixed Poisson probabilities>. Both <probabilities> refer to the same $n$-year exposure; the numerator is not a <probability> for $S_{n+1}$. For a proper <prior> and $n>0$, the integrals defining this <posterior mean> are finite because $\theta^{s+1}e^{-n\theta}$ is bounded on $[0,\infty)$.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Insert the shape-two gamma <prior> into the integral from part (ii). The <gamma function> integral gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
p_n(s)
&=\frac{n^s\lambda^2}{s!}\int_0^\infty\theta^{s+1}e^{-(n+\lambda)\theta}\,d\theta\\
&=\frac{n^s\lambda^2\Gamma(s+2)}{s!(n+\lambda)^{s+2}}\\
&=\boxed{(s+1)\left(\frac\lambda{n+\lambda}\right)^2
\left(\frac n{n+\lambda}\right)^s},\qquad s=0,1,\ldots.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore $S_n$ has a <negative binomial distribution> with shape two and success <probability> $\lambda/(n+\lambda)$, using the failures-before-two-successes convention. This is the <Poisson-gamma mixture>. The identity $\sum_{s\geq0}(s+1)z^s=(1-z)^{-2}$ verifies that the <probabilities> sum to one.

Its adjacent <probability> ratio is
$$
\frac{p_n(s+1)}{p_n(s)}=\frac{s+2}{s+1}\frac n{n+\lambda}.
$$
Substituting in the <posterior> formula gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X_{n+1}\mid S_n=s]=\frac{s+2}{n+\lambda},}
$$
exactly the estimate from part (i). The equality is required by sufficiency, and the direct computation confirms that the two negative-binomial and gamma parameter conventions have been used consistently.
